From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 15:34:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] SystemServiceRegistry: report each missing system service
 once

onServiceNotFound() wtf()s for every failed lookup made by a system-uid
process. A wtf re-enters ActivityManagerService, which records it with

    dbox = mContext.getSystemService(DropBoxManager.class);

and while DropBoxManagerService is not published yet that lookup fails
too -- landing back in onServiceNotFound(), which wtf()s again, which AMS
records the same way. AMS already guards its own half ("Bail early if not
published yet", try/catch plus a null check), but the wtf is emitted as a
side effect inside the lookup, before AMS ever gets to catch anything, so
the guard cannot break the cycle.

It needs one wtf before DropBox is up to start. On this device
DisplayManagerService.getStoredUserPreferredModeLocked() asks for
UserManager during display setup, before UserManagerService publishes
"user" -- four genuine misses, which then became 189514 wtfs in a single
boot. AMS's handler saturated, so system_server never got as far as
startOtherServices to register DropBox, which would have ended the loop;
the watchdog killed it at 66s with "Blocked in handler on main thread",
and init rebooted after the fourth zygote death. Every boot.

Report each distinct missing service once and log a warning thereafter.
The first occurrence still gets the full wtf with its stack, so nothing
that made this diagnosable is lost.

---
 .../android/app/SystemServiceRegistry.java    | 20 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/core/java/android/app/SystemServiceRegistry.java b/core/java/android/app/SystemServiceRegistry.java
index cc0e7b5cb7fa..7089f0fe9acd 100644
--- a/core/java/android/app/SystemServiceRegistry.java
+++ b/core/java/android/app/SystemServiceRegistry.java
@@ -336,8 +336,11 @@ import com.android.internal.telecom.TelecomDependencies;
 import com.android.internal.util.Preconditions;
 import com.android.modules.utils.ravenwood.RavenwoodHelper;
 
+import java.util.Collections;
+import java.util.HashSet;
 import java.util.Map;
 import java.util.Objects;
+import java.util.Set;
 
 /**
  * Manages all of the system services that can be returned by {@link Context#getSystemService}.
@@ -2895,10 +2898,25 @@ public final class SystemServiceRegistry {
         // We're mostly interested in tracking down long-lived core system
         // components that might stumble if they obtain bad references; just
         // emit a tidy log message for normal apps
-        if (android.os.Process.myUid() < android.os.Process.FIRST_APPLICATION_UID) {
+        //
+        // lineage-24.0: report each missing service once. A Log.wtf here re-enters
+        // ActivityManagerService, which records the report through
+        // getSystemService(DropBoxManager.class) -- and while DropBoxManagerService is not
+        // published yet that lookup lands back here and wtf()s again, which AMS records the same
+        // way. On this device four genuine misses (DisplayManagerService asking for UserManager
+        // during display setup, before UserManagerService publishes) turned into 189514 wtfs,
+        // saturating AMS's handler so system_server never reached startOtherServices to register
+        // DropBox, and the watchdog killed it at 66s, every boot. Capping the wtf keeps the
+        // diagnostic for the first occurrence and stops the feedback loop.
+        if (android.os.Process.myUid() < android.os.Process.FIRST_APPLICATION_UID
+                && sReportedMissingServices.add(e.getMessage())) {
             Log.wtf(TAG, e.getMessage(), e);
         } else {
             Log.w(TAG, e.getMessage());
         }
     }
+
+    /** lineage-24.0: messages already reported by {@link #onServiceNotFound}. @hide */
+    private static final Set<String> sReportedMissingServices =
+            Collections.synchronizedSet(new HashSet<>());
 }
